B.RV=s(["Used for property let to tenants (shops, offices, blocks of flats).","Steps: gross rent, less outgoings = net income, then multiply by Years' Purchase (YP).","YP is the multiplier from the investor's required yield. It is taken from yields on comparable sales.","A freehold in perpetuity uses YP = 1 \xf7 yield. A leasehold for a fixed term uses the annuity form.","Limitation: the answer depends heavily on the yield chosen."],t.s)
B.UI=s(["Capital value = Net income \xd7 YP","YP (in perpetuity) = 1 \xf7 i  (i = yield as a decimal)","YP (for n years) = (1 \u2212 (1 + i)^\u2212n) \xf7 i"],t.s)
B.Xg=new A.eM("Investment method","Values income-producing property by capitalising its net income.",B.RV,B.UI,"Net income = \u20a62,000,000, yield = 8%.\nYP = 1 \xf7 0.08 = 12.5\nCapital value = 2,000,000 \xd7 12.5 = \u20a625,000,000.")
B.Ua=s(["Best when many recent, similar sales exist (e.g. houses and land in one area).","Adjust each comparable for time of sale, location, size, condition, tenure and services.","Common units of comparison: price per square metre, per plot or per room.","Limitation: reliable sales data can be hard to find. Poor comparables give poor values."],t.s)
B.Uh=s(["Price per m\xb2 = Sale price \xf7 Area of comparable","Value = Adjusted price per m\xb2 \xd7 Area of subject property"],t.s)
B.X9=new A.eM("Comparative (market) method","Values a property from recent sales of similar properties.",B.Ua,B.Uh,"A comparable sold for \u20a660,000,000 on 300 m\xb2.\nPrice per m\xb2 = \u20a6200,000.\nSubject property = 250 m\xb2, so value \u2248 200,000 \xd7 250 = \u20a650,000,000, before other adjustments.")
B.UV=s(["Used where value comes from trading: hotels, cinemas, filling stations, event centres.","Start from gross receipts, deduct working expenses to get the net profit, then deduct the tenant's share (their reward for running the business).","What remains is the rent the property can bear. Capitalise it with YP.","Use several years' accounts, not one year.","Limitation: depends on the quality of the business accounts."],t.s)
B.Sd=s(["Net profit = Gross receipts \u2212 Working expenses","Rent = Net profit \u2212 Tenant's share","Capital value = Rent \xd7 YP"],t.s)
B.Xb=new A.eM("Profits method","Values trade-based property from the profit its business earns.",B.UV,B.Sd,"Gross receipts = \u20a630,000,000; working expenses = \u20a618,000,000.\nNet profit = \u20a612,000,000. Tenant's share = \u20a64,000,000.\nRent = \u20a68,000,000. With YP = 10, value = \u20a680,000,000.")
B.SP=s(["Used for land with development potential, or buildings to be redeveloped.","GDV (gross development value) is the expected value of the completed development.","Deduct all development costs and the developer's profit. What remains is the amount available to pay for the land.","Very sensitive to small changes in costs, GDV and profit margin, so run low, expected and high cases.","Check planning permission and approvals before relying on the result."],t.s)
B.Sq=s(["Residual land value = GDV \u2212 (Construction cost + Professional fees + Finance cost + Developer's profit + Other costs)"],t.s)
B.X3=new A.eM("Residual method","Values development land or a site from what a project would be worth.",B.SP,B.Sq,"GDV = \u20a6500M; construction = \u20a6250M; fees = \u20a625M; finance = \u20a630M; developer's profit (20% of GDV) = \u20a6100M.\nResidual = 500 \u2212 250 \u2212 25 \u2212 30 \u2212 100 = \u20a695M.")
B.UT=s(["Used for special properties with no market or income: schools, churches, hospitals, public buildings.","Estimate what it would cost to rebuild the building today, then deduct depreciation for age, wear and obsolescence.","Add the value of the land, usually found with the comparative method.","Limitation: cost is not always value. Overbuilt or unwanted buildings are worth less than their cost."],t.s)
B.Sy=s(["Value = Land value + (Replacement cost \u2212 Depreciation)","Depreciation = Replacement cost \xd7 Depreciation rate"],t.s)
B.Xa=new A.eM("Cost (contractor's) method","Values a property from the cost of replacing it, plus the land.",B.UT,B.Sy,"Land = \u20a640,000,000; replacement cost = \u20a6120,000,000; depreciation 25% = \u20a630,000,000.\nValue = 40 + (120 \u2212 30) = \u20a6130,000,000.")
